Make the CLI's CEF packaging probe see a feature enabled through `default`.

The macOS bundler copies "Chromium Embedded Framework.framework" and the five
helper .apps only when its `enabled_features` list contains `cef` (or
`tauri/cef`) — see `cef_path` in tauri-cli/src/interface/rust.rs. That list is
built by expanding every enabled feature through the app's [features] table,
but get_enabled_features returns ONLY what a feature expands TO, never the
feature's own name. So with

    [features]
    default = ["cef"]
    cef = ["dep:cef"]

expanding `default` yields `dep:cef`, and the literal `cef` never appears. The
probe misses, cef_path is None, and the bundler silently emits an .app holding
the main executable and no CEF payload whatsoever — which then panics in
cef::library_loader the moment macOS launches it. Passing `--features cef` does
not help: the name is dropped there too.

This is why the app bundled correctly before it moved to the standalone
tauri-runtime-cef crate: the `tauri` dependency then carried
features = ["cef", ...], and dependency features reach the list down a
different path (mapped as `tauri/<feature>`), so the probe matched `tauri/cef`.
Published tauri has no `cef` feature, so that route is gone for good.

Emit a nested feature's own name alongside its expansion. `default = ["cef"]`
then becomes the packaging signal app/src-tauri/Cargo.toml already documents.
Dev is deliberately unaffected: ops/dev.sh builds with `--no-default-features
--features dev-cef`, and `dev-cef` still expands only to `dep:cef`, so it keeps
dodging this probe and running through our own bundle runner.

